-- std/Io.bang — host IO as ordinary effects (ADR-0104, host-io-design §1).
--
-- These are PLAIN `effect` decls: `import Io` names them, `with Io_Console as c { … }`
-- installs a handler, `c.print(msg)` performs an op. Nothing kernel-new — IO is a
-- paradigm-as-library (ADR-0084, the moat).
--
-- WHY A MODULE, NOT THE PRELUDE (least-authority, os-inspiration §1): a program must
-- `import Io` to name these effects, so its `main` row is the capability manifest
-- (ADR-0093 D5) — the opposite of ambient authority. A dependency cannot quietly acquire
-- IO: the acquisition would change its signature and every caller's row with it.
--
-- SEPARATE LABELS, NOT ONE `effect IO { … }` (row-attenuation): each label is a
-- separately-grantable capability. `--allow=Console` grants Console without Clock; the
-- driver (Main.lean) joins resolver-owned bundled-Io provenance to allocated labels (labels
-- are `4 + decl-index`, not fixed — ADR-0104). Matching user names/ops are never host identity.
--
-- PAYLOADS ARE SENDABLE (ADR-0101 G7): `Str` is μ-data, `Int`/`Unit` are ground, so every
-- op payload and result serializes into the record/replay trace (host-io-design §3). An op
-- carrying a thunk or a cap would be an escape channel out of the handler AND would have no
-- faithful trace serialization — the Sendable constraint keeps both closed.

-- Console — line-oriented terminal IO. `print` writes a line to stdout (returns unit);
-- `readLine` reads a line from stdin. Both Sendable (`Str`/`Unit`).
pub effect Console { print : Str -> Unit, readLine : Unit -> Str }

-- Clock — wall-clock reads. `now` returns milliseconds since the epoch. The canonical
-- "same program, different answer per run": its recorded trace is one `Int` per read, and
-- replay reproduces THAT run's timestamps (host-io-design §3, honest-limits).
pub effect Clock { now : Unit -> Int }

-- Fs — filesystem access, Deno-shaped (host-io-design §1; ADR-0104 §Scope "next" tier, now
-- landed by the hostio-widen lane). Three ops, all Sendable in AND out:
--
--   readFile  : Str -> Str          -- read a whole file's UTF-8 contents into a Str
--   writeFile : Str * Str -> Unit    -- write a body (2nd) to a path (1st); overwrites/creates
--   exists    : Str -> Int           -- 1 if the path exists, 0 otherwise (Int-as-Bool; v1 has
--                                       no ergonomic sum-typed carrier for a Bool result yet)
--
-- WHY THESE SHAPES (the v1 type-vocabulary constraint, host-io-design §1):
--   * `readFile`/`writeFile` carry only `Str` (μ-data, Sendable) and `Unit` — no FD or handle
--     ever crosses (FD-as-payload is REJECTED, ADR-0104: an opaque host resource in a value
--     is a non-Sendable escape channel + has no faithful trace serialization). The whole-file
--     read/write surface needs no handle at all — the path IS the capability token.
--   * `exists` returns `Int` (0/1), NOT a `Bool`: the prelude has no auto-derived Bool carrier
--     that survives the Sendable-trace round-trip as cleanly as a ground `Int`, and every other
--     host op result is already ground Int/Str/Unit. An `Int`-as-predicate keeps the trace's
--     `result` column uniformly ground (host-io-design §3: all four fields Sendable).
--   * `writeFile`'s `Str * Str` PAIR payload is the first multi-arg host op — it rides the
--     `hostPerformS .two` lowering (ADR-0104 §4 reach, already arg-arity-generic): the surface
--     `Io.writeFile(path, body)` lowers to `perform (vcap hostCapId ℓ) "writeFile" (pair p b)`,
--     and the driver's `hostServiceReal` splits the pair back into (path, body). No new seam.
--
-- DEFERRED (named, not a dead end — host-io-design §1's "honest v1 within the wall"):
--   * `listDir : Str -> [Str]` — needs an ergonomic sum/list carrier for the RESULT (a Str list)
--     that serializes cleanly into the trace; the v1 vocabulary has `μ`-lists but no derived
--     Show/parse round-trip for a list-of-Str trace field. Filed with the effect, not shipped.
--   * `readFile` on a missing path — v1 the real handler FAILS LOUD (host-io-design §3: the
--     driver's `none` → the "no host handler / IO failure" fail-loud terminal), rather than
--     returning a sum `Result Str Err`; a typed error channel wants the same sum-carrier
--     `listDir` waits on. `Io.exists(path)` is the v1 pre-check idiom.
pub effect Fs { readFile : Str -> Str, writeFile : Str * Str -> Unit, exists : Str -> Int }

-- ── SIM handlers (the DEFAULT runtime; `--env=sim`, verified-core semantics) ────────────
--
-- A handler is SYNTAX (`with L as h { … }`), not a first-class value in v1, so a sim cannot
-- be exported as a plain `let`. Instead this module documents the canonical sim clause
-- bodies; a program (or `examples/hostio-echo`) installs them at its use site. Each clause is
-- `ret`-shape (ADR-0095 D4): the ret-shape wall forbids effects-before-resume in a v1 clause.
--
-- Console sim — `print` swallows the line (returns unit); `readLine` returns a FIXED line.
-- A scripted, stateful readLine (feeding successive lines) wants carried-param UPDATE
-- (ADR-0092 D5, open) so the fixed-source form is the honest v1 sim (host-io-design §1,
-- the ndet-dst "honest v1 within the wall" discipline):
--
--     with Io_Console as c {
--       print(s)    => (),        -- swallow: the sim produces no real output
--       readLine(u) => SNil       -- a fixed empty line (or a scripted constant)
--     }
--
-- Clock sim — `now` returns a FIXED timestamp (deterministic, replayable):
--
--     with Io_Clock as k { now(u) => 0 }
--
-- Fs sim — the SAME within-the-wall discipline (host-io-design §1). A stateful in-memory file
-- MAP (writeFile updating what a later readFile returns) wants carried-param UPDATE (ADR-0092 D5,
-- open), so the honest v1 sim is FIXED-source: `readFile` returns a constant body, `exists`
-- returns a constant, `writeFile` swallows (returns unit, no persistence). A program that wants
-- record/replay determinism over REAL fs nondeterminism uses explicit `--allow=Fs` plus typed
-- `--allow-fs-read`/`--allow-fs-write` roots under `--env=real --record`, then
-- `--replay` (ADR-0104 §3) — the trace, not a sim map, is the v1 determinism source for Fs:
--
--     with Io_Fs as fs {
--       readFile(p)    => SNil,      -- a fixed (empty) body; a scripted constant otherwise
--       writeFile(pb)  => (),        -- swallow: the sim persists nothing
--       exists(p)      => 0          -- a fixed "absent" (or a scripted constant)
--     }
--
-- The REAL handlers live host-side in Main.lean's driver loop (`--env=real`), do the actual
-- IO in Lean's IO monad, and RECORD each (label,op,payload,result) row so the run can be
-- REPLAYED under these sim handlers on the pure oracle — the invariant-#1 gate (ADR-0104 §3).
